# In the Gold Fields
Tamil title: தங்க வயலில்

A field that yields gold, yet poverty dances there.

The princely state of Mysore, reputed to be highly progressive in the world of labour, has handed the Kolar Gold Fields to a white-owned company. After obtaining a considerable benefit for itself, it remains unconcerned and allows the company to treat the mine workers however it pleases.

Kolar has become a hunting ground for the profits of white capitalists, while the workers' lives have become pitiable. Three-quarters of those workers are Tamils, people of the oppressed communities. They descend thousands of feet beneath the earth, cut out the gold, bring it up, and hand it to the employer, yet those comrades remain afflicted by poverty.

The company earns profits of many crores of rupees. The Mysore state receives more than two crore rupees from it every year. Once its own stomach is full, the state is satisfied and leaves the company free to treat the workers in any manner it chooses.

For many days, the workers had presented, in the calmest manner, their demand that their wages were insufficient. There is unity and discipline among them. At this moment, every worker engaged in the Kolar mines is united, and every labour organization in the Kolar region speaks with one voice.

Though the company knew all this, it did not listen to the workers' demand. The workers therefore went on strike. The strike has continued for the past month without disorder. The highly capable manner in which the Kolar workers have conducted it has raised their standing.

Everyone who knows the nature of their work and the dangers they face accepts that their demand is just. The company cares only about profit. The princely state, meanwhile, has granted the company self-government! It concentrates solely upon placing the two crores received every year in an iron chest and locking it, content so long as no hand reaches beneath its own waistcloth.

When those workers have been on strike for a month, it is no game. It means many thousands of people are suffering, and the lives of countless people may already have been shattered.

Despite all this hardship, the company says that it no longer earns the profits it once did. The world knows very well the enormous profit made by that white-owned company from the Kolar Gold Fields. The workers' reward for labouring in the mine has been an emaciated body, disease, and damaged limbs. One need only look at them to know it.

The workers are not asking for money to install marble on the third floor of their mansion. They ask for a slight wage increase merely to save themselves, as they lie in their huts, from having the large intestine consume the small intestine in hunger.

The company that has accumulated crores upon crores of pleasure shows no sign of caring about the tears of the poor. Even fulfilling the workers' demand would not impose a mountain of expense upon it. Compared with the profits it earns, what the workers ask is exceedingly little.

Our Tamil workers strain until sinew and nerve are torn while the company makes profits in crores. The total wage increase they request would cost the company only five lakh rupees. Yet the company has no heart even for this.

The princely state displays far more enthusiasm in serving as the company's advocate than in ending the workers' misery. The strike has continued for a month, but the company will not yield. The state shows concern for maintaining law and order, but appears never to wonder whether it is a disgrace that thousands of families have struggled with hunger and suffered without work for a month.

The Mysore state enjoys a great reputation in the world. That reputation will certainly diminish as a result of the Kolar Gold Fields strike.

Why should the Mysore state not stand as guardian of the thousands of workers and compel the company to behave properly? Should it ignore the tears of thousands of workers for the sake of the smiles of a few white masters?

The world reads that Mysore has sandalwood forests, a power-generating station, industrial wealth, and a land that yields gold, and it praises Mysore. But if it reads along with that news that thousands of workers in Mysore suffer on half-empty stomachs, will the world still praise the state?

Mysore's administrators must consider this carefully. The state spends more upon horses, other animals, and flower gardens maintained for the ruler's pleasure, recreation, and sport than it spends upon the lives of the workers who mine its gold. The state cannot deny this.

It must come forward quickly to wipe away the workers' suffering. Only then will the description of the state's ruler as protector of the people be fitting. Otherwise, the belief will be confirmed that such princely rulers are display dolls in the exhibition hall of imperialism.

Mysore must come forward to protect its good name. Is it right first to let the scorpion of poverty sting the workers' lives and then complain that they are unruly, that they go on strike and damage industrial growth, and that they create disorder?

We must praise the resolve and discipline of the comrades who have endured the suffering of a strike for a month. But it would be foolish for the state to imagine that this calm will last forever. Patience has its limit. Without compelling the workers to cross that limit, it must attend to their grievances.

If the state thinks that whenever an agitation arises there is always the bullet, the world of reason will certainly condemn such a course.

Tamil Nadu is deeply distressed to hear of the suffering of the comrades in the Kolar Gold Fields. Dravidar Kazhagam branches everywhere should ask the Mysore state to resolve their grievances. Resolutions should be sent to the state, and telegrams should be dispatched to Mysore.

The state must be made to understand that Tamil Nadu stands beside those thousands of labourers.

*Dravida Nadu*, 17 March 1946.

## Anna's Ill Health: Announcement

Tamil title: அண்ணாவின் உடல்நலக்குறைவு - அறிவிப்பு

To the comrades,

Because my present ill health leaves me unable to travel outside the city for a little while longer, I ask your generous forgiveness for being unable immediately to fulfil the invitations received from the Madurai and South Arcot regions and from other comrades.

Annadurai

News item, *Dravida Nadu*, 17 March 1948.

---NOTES---
The canonical Tamil OCR block is preserved verbatim by the bilingual builder. Image 6 is an independent 1948 announcement appended to the complete 1946 Gold Fields article; both are translated and explicitly separated. “பழங்குடி மக்கள்” in this labour context is rendered conservatively as people of the oppressed communities rather than assigned a modern statutory category. The source's figures, labour conditions, and institutional claims are translated in its voice and have not been independently verified.
